{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tmofv4zdhhvphwhpUvzKq7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# SKILLER7\n2\t\n3\t*Seven Killers, One Mountain.*\n4\t\n5\t**Title.** Picked the way TASK.md says: 50 playful riffs on \"Killer7\" were brainstormed silently, then one was drawn\n6\twith a real random number generator (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`, OS entropy): index 13, **Skiller7**. It reads three\n7\tways at once (SKI + KILLER + SKILL), and the whole design leans on that. In file and folder names, GameTitle is\n8\t`Skiller7` and SOURCE is `Killer7`.\n9\t\n10\t**Pitch.** Piz Nada is a neutral micro-state on one mountain, and it has snowed there for a hundred years. On the top\n11\tfloor of its sanatorium, in Room 7, a 104-year-old former ski champion called Oma Holle lies under a mountain of\n12\tfeatherbed. When she shakes it, seven killers step out of her wardrobe. The Canton's Department of Discretion sends\n13\tthem \"guests\" to check out: agents of Mister Föhn, the warm wind that makes people laugh until they disappear. You move\n14\ton rails through a grand hotel, a ski village, a summit conference, a black ski run, a gondola and a glacier vault.\n15\tThen you stop, look through your persona's eyes, scan for the laughing, invisible Whiteouts, and shoot their Warm Spot\n16\tbefore they reach you.\n17\t\n18\t---\n19\t\n20\t## 0. How to use this plan\n21\t\n22\t- **TASK.md + PLAN.md are the whole brief.** TASK.md decides deliverables and process. This plan decides design. Where\n23\t  this plan gives a number, use it as the default and expose the important ones as live dev sliders (§7.22).\n24\t- **Everything in the game is original.** We take Killer7's *structure* (rail movement, stop-and-aim first-person\n25\t  scanning, weak-point instant kills, one body with several killer personas, a save room, a blood economy, cryptic\n26\t  hint-givers, chapters called targets, bosses with odd rules) and its *tone*. We do not use its names, characters,\n27\t  art, music, levels or look.\n28\t- **Build in this order:** a playable greybox golden path first, then systems to full depth, then content, then polish\n29\t  (§12). The scope is sized to be built whole. TASK.md forbids TODOs, placeholders and stubs, so build every system and\n30\t  piece of content in this plan.\n31\t- **Mark every engine change** in the engine files with `// S7 PATCH:` and a reason, and keep a list for the\n32\t  hand-back's Engine Feedback (§16).\n33\t\n34\t---\n35\t\n36\t## 1. At a glance\n37\t\n38\t| | |\n39\t|---|---|\n40\t| Title | **SKILLER7** (shown with that spelling; `Skiller7` in file names) |\n41\t| Tagline | Seven Killers, One Mountain |\n42\t| Source | Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005) |\n43\t| Genre | On-rails action-adventure shooter: rail movement, first-person scan-and-shoot, persona-switch puzzles, boss fights. AI Hub catalogue genre: **Shooter** |\n44\t| Perspective | Third-person cinematic rail cameras (fixed, dolly, follow and crane shots that hard-cut) while moving; **first person** while aiming; a schematic top-down map in the pause menu |\n45\t| Players | 1 in the campaign. 1–4 in **Echo Run** (AI Hub ghost mode; solo, you race your own recorded echo) |\n46\t| Length | about 45–60 min: Target 00 \"Turn-Down Service\" (about 12 min), Target 01 \"Après\" (35–45 min), epilogue. Echo Run rounds take about 2 min |\n47\t| Engine | my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** handoff (the 2D engine runs the game) plus the engine's own **3D module v0.85.2** (three.js r182, Rapier 0.19.3 from a CDN) |\n48\t| Look | The engine's native style: PS1-resolution picture (`'pixels'`), characters as pixel-art rigs drawn on cards, the 3D module's **Comic cel** filter (flat bands of light, ink lines). Stark white snow, black shadows, saturated red |\n49\t| Animation | The engine's procedural rigs (`E.Humanoid`, `E.Blob`, `E.MOVES`, poses), extended with new hats, accessories, weapons, skis and poses; first-person weapon models with the overlay pen |\n50\t| Audio | All in code: engine chip-synth songs, engine SFX voices, our own WebAudio laughter synth and Animalese-style gibberish voices |\n51\t| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, `ghost` mode (Echo Run). On its own, the game runs without the hub |\n52\t| Ship | `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html` and `exit-interview.json` in this folder. Dev files go in `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` |\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## 2. Killer7 → Skiller7: what we keep, what is ours\n57\t\n58\t| What makes Killer7 distinctive | Skiller7 (original) |\n59\t|---|---|\n60\t| Hold one button to run along fixed rails; junction icons; a quick 180° turn | Rails through hotel halls and village lanes; **downhill ski rails** with momentum and hockey stops; **lifts** (T-bar, gondola) you can shoot from; **ski jumps**; an **avalanche chase** |\n61\t| Stop, aim in first person, scan to reveal invisible laughing suicide bombers, weak spots that kill at once | **Whiteouts**: Föhn-touched people who laugh themselves invisible. You hear them, see their **footprints in the snow and their breath**, and a **Scan** pulse reveals them. Shoot the glowing **Warm Spot** and they thaw on the spot (\"THAW.\") |\n62\t| One body, seven personas, each with a gun and a field ability | **Oma Holle's Seven**: a ski patrolman, a downhill racer, a telemark aristocrat, a fur-coated tycoon, a yodeler, a press photographer and an avalanche engineer |\n63\t| A cleaner who retrieves fallen personas | **The Sweep** skis in from Room 7 and chips frozen personas out of the ice |\n64\t| A save room with a maid and a TV | **Room 7**: Sister Rosalind, the **Cuckoo Clock** (save), the **Wax Bench** (upgrades: you wax your skills), the **Wardrobe** of seven coats (persona select) |\n65\t| Thin blood heals, thick blood buys upgrades | **Red** (shared; heals and fuels skills) and **Rime** (upgrade crystals) |\n66\t| A cryptic ghost and a grotesque hint-giver | **Pim the Lift Boy** (the ghost of a 1926 lift attendant on a floating chairlift seat) and **Murmel** (a resort-mascot marmot bungeed to a ski rack: \"Gratuity is customary.\") |\n67\t| A handler on the phone, political allegory, an eternal game between two gods | **The Concierge** (hotel euphemisms for murder), the **Thaw Accord**, Oma Holle against **Mister Föhn** over a chessboard of ice |\n68\t| Cel shading, flat colour, heavy black shadows, huge type | Comic-cel filter and ink. White-on-white snow made readable by ink outlines. Red on snow. Huge title cards |\n69\t| Chapters called targets, each ending in a strange boss | **Target 00** (the Bellcaptain in an elevator shaft). **Target 01** (an oompah band you shoot on the beat; the Powder King: a laughing snowman colossus, a slalom duel, and an interview on a stuck chairlift) |\n70\t\n71\t---\n72\t\n73\t## 3. Deliverables, folders, ground rules\n74\t\n75\t**At the end, this folder holds:** TASK.md, STAGE.md (if present), PLAN.md, the built game\n76\t`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html`, `exit-interview.json` beside it, and the dev folder\n77\t`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` with every source, tool and test. If your TASK.md gives the\n78\tnames with a different model or date, use your TASK.md's names (GameTitle `Skiller7`, SOURCE `Killer7`).\n79\t\n80\t**Shared machine rules (from TASK.md):**\n81\t- Read only the files you need. Other agents share this directory, the machine's ports and its processes.\n82\t- Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. Only the AI Hub harness needs a server. Start it with\n83\t  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (it prints the port), check that the served page really is your game\n84\t  (its `<title>` starts with \"Skiller7\"), and stop it by its own PID. Never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever\n85\t  holds a port.\n86\t- `/tmp` on this machine is a small tmpfs and may be full. Keep large files (vendor copies, screenshots, logs) inside\n87\t  the dev folder, and run Node and Playwright with `TMPDIR` pointing at a `tmp/` folder inside it (Chromium's\n88\t  profiles go there). Playwright's Chromium 1243 is already installed in `~/.cache/ms-playwright`.\n89\t- Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n90\t- One test command prints only failures and a one-line count (§14). Rerun it only after changing code it covers. Use\n91\t  screenshots only for what a script can't check.\n92\t\n93\t---\n94\t\n95\t## 4. Tech stack and pinned sources\n96\t\n97\t**Pinned commits** (the repositories move daily; pin them for reproducibility):\n98\t- my-3d2dge: `4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72` (engine v0.85.2). Raw files come from\n99\t  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72/<path>`\n100\t- ai-hub: `f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e` (session client 1.4.0). Raw files come from\n101\t  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e/<path>`\n102\t\n103\t`tools/fetch-deps.mjs` downloads these once, idempotently:\n104\t\n105\t| What | Repo path | Dev-folder path | Shipped? |\n106\t|---|---|---|---|\n107\t| **The whole handoff folder**, layout kept so its tools, kits and tests run unchanged: the 2D engine (full, readable, every module: story, rpg, songs, arcade…), `modules/`, `kits/`, `game/`, `tools/`, `docs/`, `src/assets/` (the store), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `package.json` | `dist/handoff/` (a sparse checkout at the pinned commit, or the GitHub tree API plus raw downloads) | `engine/` | only `engine/my-3d2dge.js` is inlined |\n108\t| Asset store sets we use | `dist/handoff/src/assets/sets/{kenney-holiday,kenney-furniture,kaykit-furniture-bits,kenney-nature}.js` | `engine/src/assets/sets/` (part of the copy above) | inlined: only the entries we use, cut with `engine/tools/asset-set.mjs` |\n109\t| 3D module (ES module, header = its manual) | `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | inlined as a module |\n110\t| 3D reference slices (read, don't ship) | `src/starter3d/00-shell.js`, `30-shooter.js`, `40-horror.js`, `60-flight.js` | `ref/` | no |\n111\t| three.js r182 (tests only) | `vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js`, `three.core.min.js`, `three.tsl.min.js` | `vendor/three-0.182.0/` | no (CDN) |\n112\t| Rapier 0.19.3 (tests only) | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js` | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/` | no (CDN) |\n113\t| AI Hub client | ai-hub `lib/hub/session.js` | `vendor/aihub/session.js` | inlined **unmodified**, first script in `<head>` |\n114\t| AI Hub harness (tests only) | ai-hub `lib/hub/harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` | `vendor/aihub/` | no |\n115\t\n116\t**Runtime import map (shipped).** All four URLs were checked: HTTP 200, `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`.\n117\t```json\n118\t{ \"imports\": {\n119\t  \"three\":        \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n120\t  \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n121\t  \"three/tsl\":    \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.min.js\",\n122\t  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs\" } }\n123\t```\n124\t`three.webgpu.min.js` imports `./three.core.min.js` beside it on the CDN. Rapier's compat build embeds its wasm, so\n125\tnothing else is fetched. Inline module scripts and import maps work from `file://`, so the single file plays when\n126\tdouble-clicked (online). The AI Hub localizes these engine URLs when it ingests a game (its shelf has three 0.180–0.186.1\n127\tand Rapier 0.19.3). Tests route the URLs to the `vendor/` copies, so they never depend on the network.\n128\t\n129\t**Tooling:** Node 24 (on the machine), Python 3 (the harness server only), Playwright **1.63.0** (pinned as in the\n130\thandoff's package.json) with `npx playwright install chromium`. No other libraries, no bundler: the build script\n131\tconcatenates (§5.8).\n132\t\n133\t**Verified while planning** (a throwaway spike, not part of the build): one HTML file holding the 2D engine (classic\n134\tscript), the 3D module (inline module) and this import map, opened from `file://` in headless Chromium 1243 through\n135\tPlaywright 1.63.0, loaded three r182 and Rapier from jsdelivr and booted `E.World3D` with no page errors. Headless\n136\tChromium has no WebGPU, so both `?backend=webgl` and the default ran on WebGL 2. A level with `ground: 'snow'`, a\n137\tHumanoid on a card with `pose: false` that our code `place()`d and `rig.update()`d every step, a `'fixed'` camera set in\n138\t`draw`, `world.glow` (it already reads as a Warm Spot), `world.project` and the `cel` filter all rendered as expected.\n139\tTwo lessons: an actor's position reads back from Rapier in float32 (about 3e-6 off), so the sim keeps its own positions\n140\tand never reads actors; and `'fixed'` cameras don't cut walls, so every shot must sit in open space (§7.2).\n141\t\n142\t**Why the 3D module.** Killer7 is cinematic third-person rails plus first-person aiming. The handoff's 2D views can't do\n143\tfirst person. The engine's own 3D module (the same engine and release, documented as its 3D side) gives exactly what\n144\twe need: `'fixed'` cameras we pose ourselves, a first-person overlay for the gun, `world.project` for the HUD, fog,\n145\tlights, shadows, terrain for ski runs, the 2D engine's rigs drawn as cards, and a built-in **Comic cel** filter. The 2D\n146\tengine still runs everything else: scenes, the fixed-step clock, input and rebinding, the pause menu and settings,\n147\tsaves, the Story and Dialog modules, the music clock, the HUD font and widgets.\n148\t\n149\t---\n150\t\n151\t## 5. Architecture\n152\t\n153\t### 5.1 The built page, in order\n154\t\n155\t```html\n156\t<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head>\n157\t<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n158\t<script>/*! aihub-session 1.4.0 … pasted unmodified … */</script>       <!-- first script, right after charset -->\n159\t<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n160\t<title>Skiller7 — Seven Killers, One Mountain</title>\n161\t<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Seven killers share one body and one mountain. Ride the rails, stop, scan for the laughing invisible Whiteouts and shoot the warm spot.\">\n162\t<meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#d81e2a\">\n163\t<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"data:image/svg+xml,…a red 7 on white…\">\n164\t<meta name=\"hub:source\" content=\"Killer7\">\n165\t<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n166\t<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1–4\">\n167\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n168\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n169\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n170\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n171\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Fastest down the Black Run wins; crits cut time\">\n172\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Hold W/✕ ski · S/○ stop · RMB/L2 aim · LMB/R2 shoot · Space/L1 scan\">\n173\t<script type=\"importmap\">{ …§4… }</script>\n174\t<style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#000;overflow:hidden} #stage{position:relative;width:100%;height:100%}\n175\t#view{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;pointer-events:none;z-index:0} #screen{position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;image-rendering:pixelated;z-index:1;background:transparent}</style>\n176\t</head><body>\n177\t<!-- E.bootSplash('SKILLER7') markup: the splash shows while scripts load -->\n178\t<div id=\"stage\"><canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas></div>\n179\t<script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js v0.85.2 (with S7 PATCHes) */</script>\n180\t<script>/* the Skiller7 bundle: src + content, classic script, defines globalThis.S7, starts nothing */</script>\n181\t<script type=\"module\">/* engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js v0.85.2: imports three and Rapier through the import map */</script>\n182\t<script type=\"module\">S7.start({ World3D: My3D2dge.World3D });</script>\n183\t<script>/* watchdog: if S7.started is still false after 20 s, show \"The 3D libraries didn't load. Check the connection and reload.\" with a Reload button; in a session, call AIHub.fail(that message) */</script>\n184\t</body></html>\n185\t```\n186\tThe build escapes every `</script` inside inlined code as `<\\/script`.\n187\t\n188\t### 5.2 Sim and presenter\n189\t\n190\t```\n191\t            game.input ──► Intent layer ◄── Bot (attract, tests)\n192\t                               │ intents (move, turn, branch, aim yaw/pitch deltas, fire, scan, skill, interact…)\n193\t                               ▼\n194\t  ┌──────────────── SIM (deterministic, fixed step 1/120 s, runs in Node) ────────────────┐\n195\t  │ rails · walker · camera director · personas · weapons · skills · whiteouts · combat  │\n196\t  │ scan · bosses · economy · items · gates · story/beats · saves · results · echo run    │\n197\t  └──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────┘\n198\t                     state (read only)                   events (S7.bus)\n199\t                                 ▼                              ▼\n200\t        Presenter3D (browser) / NullPresenter (Node)      Audio · UI widgets · HUD\n201\t        World3D worlds, actors, rigs, pen, glows,         (headless-safe; audio no-ops\n202\t        lights, FX, first-person overlay, camera          without an AudioContext)\n203\t```\n204\t- **The sim** owns all game state. It uses only the 2D engine (`E.Path`, `E.rng`, `E.Story`, `E.Dialog`, `E.Menu`,\n205\t  `game.input`, `game.audio.pos`, `game.saves`, `game.script`, `game.tween`) and our own math. It never touches three.js,\n206\t  the DOM or `E.World3D`, and it never reads anything back from the presenter.\n207\t- **The presenter** turns state into pictures. It has two hooks: `step(dt)` in the play scene's update after\n208\t  `sim.update` (mirror actors, `rig.update`, `world.step`) and `draw(r)` (pen, glows, lights, `world.draw(r)`, then the\n209\t  HUD). **NullPresenter** has the same interface and does nothing; Node tests use it.\n210\t- **Mouse picking goes through intents.** The UI projects world anchors with `world.project` and turns a hover or a\n211\t  click into an intent (`branch = 2`, `interact = 'murmel'`). The sim also keeps its own simple projection (from the\n212\t  camera director's pose) for stick-to-branch mapping, so headless play needs no presenter.\n213\t- Level geometry exists twice from one source: the presenter builds `World3D` from the area's rows or terrain\n214\t  function, and the sim builds a `LevelGrid` from the same data for line of sight and paths.\n215\t\n216\t### 5.3 Source tree (dev folder)\n217\t\n218\t```\n219\tSkiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/\n220\t  package.json            { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { playwright: \"1.63.0\" },\n221\t                            scripts: { build: \"node tools/build.mjs\", test: \"node tests/run.mjs\" } }\n222\t  engine/                 a copy of dist/handoff/ (my-3d2dge.js patched; kits/, tools/, src/assets/…) + my-3d2dge-3d.js (§4)\n223\t  vendor/                 three-0.182.0/, rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/, aihub/               (§4)\n224\t  ref/                    3D starter slices, for reading\n225\t  src/\n226\t    00-core.js        S7 namespace, VERSION, S7.bus, registries (S7.area/persona/weapon/enemy/item/beat/song/voice),\n227\t                      named E.rng streams ('spawn','spread','laugh','fx'), units (M = 16 world units = 1 m)\n228\t    01-math.js        vectors, ray–sphere, ray–capsule, segment distance, angles, easing helpers\n229\t    02-tuning.js      TUNING table {key: {def, min, max, step, label, group}}, live values, reset, difficulty multipliers\n230\t    05-input.js       the S7 input preset, intent layer (human and bot), last-device tracking, idle timer,\n231\t                      touch-only detection, mouse modes (look / pointer), stick flick detection\n232\t    10-levelgrid.js   LevelGrid from level rows and legend (floor and wall heights, pits, stairs, platforms),\n233\t                      terrain sampler, line of sight (tile DDA with heights / terrain ray-march), BFS flow fields\n234\t    11-rails.js       RailGraph (nodes, edges as E.Path), Walker (walk, ski, lift, jump, stairs), junctions,\n235\t                      gate checks, spot proximity\n236\t    12-camera.js      CameraDirector: shots, cuts, blends, aim transition, overrides, shake; sim-side projection\n237\t    20-personas.js    roster state, HP, stats, availability, switching, frozen/fallen\n238\t    21-weapons.js     fire cycles, magazines, reloads, spread, recoil, hitscan/cone/lob kinds, effective range\n239\t    22-skills.js      flare, tuck, longlook, makeitrain + buyout, echo, flash, charge, rescue\n240\t    30-whiteouts.js   enemy types, behaviours, warm spots, tells (footprints, breath), laugh scheduling,\n241\t                      spawner, waves\n242\t    31-combat.js      damage pipeline; hits on enemies, props and targets; crits; explosions; projectiles\n243\t                      (snowballs, suitcases, medals, shells); drops and pickup magnet\n244\t    32-scan.js        scan pulse, reveal timers, laugh-direction cues\n245\t    33-bosses.js      boss controllers: grau, jollyfour, hansi (with phases)\n246\t    40-economy.js     Red, Rime, healing, Wax Bench costs and effects\n247\t    41-items.js       inventory, key items, collectibles, Dossier entries\n248\t    42-gates.js       gate kinds: rope, jump, farbell, buyout, echowall, crystal, exposure, codelock, cornice,\n249\t                      serac, icelock, edelweiss, key\n250\t    50-story.js       E.Story setup, people (speaker ↔ rig ↔ voice), flags, objectives, beats (game.script),\n251\t                      title cards, letterbox\n252\t    51-npcs.js        Murmel hints, Pim appearances, phones, frozen tableaux\n253\t    52-room7.js       entering and leaving, the clock, the Wax Bench, the Wardrobe, the bed, the telephone\n254\t    60-save.js        serialize/deserialize, game.saves wiring, autosave, versions and migrate\n255\t    61-results.js     per-target stats and ranks\n256\t    70-audio.js       buses, song table, SFX definitions, positional helper, music state (layers, cues), ambience\n257\t    71-voice.js       Animalese voice synth and its dialog hookup\n258\t    72-laugh.js       laughter synth (styles) and positional emitters\n259\t    80-ui-kit.js      canvas widget kit: focus lists, buttons, sliders, toggles, tabs, grids, glyph prompts;\n260\t                      pad, keys and mouse (hover focuses, click activates, drag moves sliders)\n261\t    81-hud.js         the in-play HUD\n262\t    82-screens.js     title, Featherbed ring, junction markers, Wax Bench, Wardrobe, map, Dossier and items,\n263\t                      code lock, target cards, results, game over, About/Credits, touch-only notice,\n264\t                      attract overlay\n265\t    83-shell.js       a themed E.Shell (pause, settings, controls, help, saves) and its pause items\n266\t    84-dev.js         dev mode panel and dev overlays\n267\t    85-bot.js         the bot: route runner, rail navigation, combat (used by attract mode and tests)\n268\t    86-attract.js     attract director: vignettes, cheats, any-input exit\n269\t    87-hub.js         AI Hub session flow, Echo Run networking, solo echo recording\n270\t    88-echorun.js     Echo Run rules: course, timing, penalties, results\n271\t    90-present3d.js   Presenter3D: a world per area, actor mirroring, rigs, accessories, pen drawing,\n272\t                      glows, lights, camera\n273\t    91-fx3d.js        snowfall, footprint and red-snow decals, breath, scan ring, splashes, flurry transition\n274\t    92-firstperson.js weapon models and their animations on the overlay pen\n275\t    93-present-null.js NullPresenter\n276\t    95-scenes.js      scenes: title, attract, play (Room 7 is a mode of play), results, credits, gameover,\n277\t                      echorun, hub\n278\t    99-start.js       S7.start(opts): new E.Game, boot steps, scenes, game.debug\n279\t  content/\n280\t    tuning.js personas.js weapons.js enemies.js items.js shapes.js things3d.js songs.js voices.js\n281\t    attract.js echorun.js\n282\t    areas/  room7.js 00A-lobby.js 00B-thirdfloor.js 01A-village.js 01B-palais.js 01C-blackrun.js\n283\t            01D-gondola.js 01E-archive.js 01F-summit.js\n284\t    story/  common.js t00.js t01.js epilogue.js barks.js\n285\t  tools/   build.mjs  validate-content.mjs  fetch-deps.mjs\n286\t  tests/   run.mjs  lib/node-harness.mjs  lib/browser-harness.mjs  node/*.test.mjs  browser/*.test.mjs\n287\t           routes/golden.js (the playthrough's route)  out/ (screenshots, logs, the hub copy; never shipped)\n288\t```\n289\t`content/things3d.js` only defines `S7.things3d = W3 => { W3.THINGS.chandelier = (pen, t, time) => …; … }`, and\n290\tPresenter3D calls it in its init, so nothing there runs in Node. Everything else in `content/` is plain data and runs\n291\tanywhere.\n292\t\n293\t### 5.4 The play loop and the mode machine\n294\t\n295\tThe play scene (`pausable: true`, `touch: []`):\n296\t```\n297\tupdate(dt):  if (ui.modal) { ui.modal.update(dt); return; }   // dev panel, Wax Bench, map… freeze the sim\n298\t             intents = Intent.read(game.input | bot)            // one place turns devices into intents\n299\t             sim.update(dt, intents)                            // everything in §7, in a fixed order\n300\t             presenter.step(dt)                                 // mirror, rigs, world.step (browser only)\n301\tdraw(r):     presenter.draw(r)                                  // world.draw, then the HUD and screens\n302\t```\n303\t`sim.update` order: modes → walker → camera director → skills → weapons and projectiles → whiteouts → scan and\n304\treveals → combat resolution → pickups → economy → bosses → triggers and waves → story and beats → results clock →\n305\temit events.\n306\t\n307\t**Modes** (one at a time; `S7.sim.mode`):\n308\t\n309\t| Mode | Enter | Exit | Notes |\n310\t|---|---|---|---|\n311\t| `rail` | default | — | move, turn, interact, heal, skill, ring, aim |\n312\t| `junction` | arrive at a node with 3+ edges | a branch is chosen and move is held | the walker is stopped; branch markers show |\n313\t| `aim` | aim held (or toggled) in rail/lift mode, or on a ski edge after a hockey stop | aim released | first person; the walker is stopped except on lifts and `aimOnTheMove` edges |\n314\t| `ski` | on a `ski` edge | leave the edge | automatic downhill movement; move = tuck, turn = hockey stop |\n315\t| `lift` | board a lift node | the end node | automatic movement; aiming allowed |\n316\t| `talk` | interact with an NPC, phone or tableau | the dialog ends | sim frozen except ambience |\n317\t| `ring` | hold ring | release (switch) | time × 0.15 |\n318\t| `switching` | a persona is chosen | 0.45 s later | invulnerable |\n319\t| `cutscene` | a beat script takes the camera | the script ends | skippable with start/pause → \"Skip scene?\" |\n320\t| `frozen` | the active persona's HP reaches 0 | 1.2 s later → `rescue` | the freeze animation |\n321\t| `rescue` | after `frozen` | the statue is chipped free | you play the Sweep |\n322\t| `gameover` | the Sweep's HP reaches 0 | Continue or Quit | |\n323\t| `modal` | a canvas screen opens | it closes | the sim is frozen |\n324\t\n325\t### 5.5 Data formats (the designer's tools)\n326\t\n327\tAll content is data in `content/`, checked by `tools/validate-content.mjs` (§14.7). **Units in area data:** x and y\n328\tin **tiles** (1 tile = 1 m = 16 world units; row 0 is north, column 0 is west), z in **metres**. The loader multiplies\n329\tby 16. Use `.5` for tile centres.\n330\t\n331\t```js\n332\tS7.area('00A', {\n333\t  title: 'GRAND HOTEL NADA', sub: 'LOBBY', target: 0, music: 'hotel', ambience: 'roomtone',\n334\t  world: {                                   // given to new E.World3D(game, …) and parsed by the sim's LevelGrid\n335\t    level: { rows: [/* 28 strings of 40 chars */], legend: { /* World3D legend: floor, wall, pillar, stairs,\n336\t             platform, pit, torch, mark, shape */ }, ground: 'tiles', colors: { a: '#e8e4dc', b: '#2a2226', size: 2 },\n337\t             side: 'block', color: '#3a2a2e' },\n338\t    // or terrain: { size, height: (x, y) => z /* world units, pure and shared with the sim */, color, rock, far }\n339\t    sky: '#05040a', fog: { color: '#0a0608', near: 40, far: 420 }, ambient: ['#3a2a3a', '#120a0e', 1.2],\n340\t    torchLights: 4, torchShadows: 1, lights: 6, glowLights: 6, overlay: { fov: 62 }, look: 'card',\n341\t    filter: { bands: 3, ink: 1, inkW: 1.5, punch: 1.4 },  // no `look` and no `res` here: options given to a new world\n342\t    camera: { mode: 'fixed', near: 1 }                    // override the player's 3D settings, so they come from §7.21\n343\t  },\n344\t  nodes: {                                   // t: [x, y, z?]; z defaults to the floor or terrain height there\n345\t    r7: { t: [33.5, 20.5], kind: 'room7' },  j1: { t: [28.5, 20.5], kind: 'junction' },\n346\t    x1: { t: [20.5, 2.5, 6], kind: 'exit', label: 'STAIRS', to: ['00B', 'x1'], needs: { item: 'servicekey' } }\n347\t  },\n348\t  edges: [\n349\t    { id: 'r7-j1', a: 'r7', b: 'j1', kind: 'walk',  // walk | ski | lift | jump | stairs; via: [[x, y, z?], …] bends\n350\t      via: [[31, 20.8]], cams: [{ s: [0, 1], shot: 'fixed', at: [36, 24, 3.2], look: 'hero', fov: 50 }] },\n351\t    { id: 'j3-g1', a: 'j3', b: 'g1', kind: 'walk', gate: 'rope', cams: [ … ] }\n352\t  ],\n353\t  gates:    { rope: { kind: 'buyout', price: 20, who: 'rurik', label: 'MEMBERS ONLY' } },\n354\t  spots:    [ { id: 'murmel', node: 's1', kind: 'npc', who: 'murmel', talk: 'murmel_00A' },\n355\t              { id: 'pin1', edge: 'j2-j4', s: .7, kind: 'pickup', item: 'pin1' },\n356\t              { id: 'phone_bar', node: 'b1', kind: 'phone', talk: 'concierge_bar' } ],\n357\t  spawns:   { e1: [12, 6], e2: [14, 6] },        // tile coordinates of spawn points\n358\t  waves:    { lobby1: { spawn: [['giggler', 'e1', 0], ['giggler', 'e2', 1.5]], until: 'clear' } },\n359\t  triggers: [ { id: 'tut_aim', edge: 'j1-j2', s: .6, once: true, beat: 't00_first_whiteout' },\n360\t              { id: 'w_elev', node: 'j4', once: true, wave: 'lobby1' } ],\n361\t  props:    [ ['shape', 'reception-desk', 31, 18, 0, { facing: Math.PI }], ['thing', 'chandelier', 20, 14, 5.6],\n362\t              ['light', 20, 14, 5.2, { color: '#ffd8a0', reach: 18, power: 1.2, flicker: .05 }] ],\n363\t  blocks:   [ [x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1] ]          // extra line-of-sight blockers (in tiles/metres): desks, cabins\n364\t});\n365\t```\n366\t- **Camera shots** (per edge, in `cams`; `rcams` when walking b→a, else the same list). Every shot has `s: [s0, s1]`, its\n367\t  share of the edge's length; shots must cover [0, 1].\n368\t  - `fixed`: `at`, `look` ('hero' | [x, y, z]), `lookUp` (metres above the feet, default 1.1), `fov`.\n369\t  - `dolly`: `path` (its own points), the camera slides along it as the hero moves through s0..s1; `look`, `fov`.\n370\t  - `follow`: `back`, `side`, `up`, `ahead` (metres), `fov`, `lag` (s). The validator's default for an uncovered\n371\t    range is `follow { back: 3.6, side: 1.1, up: 1.9, ahead: 2.5, fov: 55, lag: .12 }`.\n372\t  - `crane`: `at`, `zFrom`, `zTo` (metres over s0..s1), `look`, `fov`.\n373\t  - `pov`: forced first person (a dramatic corridor).\n374\t  - `blend`: seconds of ease between this shot and the last; the default is 0, a hard cut (the Killer7 way).\n375\t- **Beats** (`S7.beat(id, function* (G) { … })`) are `game.script` generators: dialogs (`yield G.say(node)`),\n376\t  camera overrides (`yield G.cam(shot, seconds)`), waits, spawns, flags, cards (`yield G.card('TARGET 00', …)`). The\n377\t  story's nodes and every line of text live in `content/story/*.js` as `E.Story` nodes.\n378\t- **Personas, weapons, enemies, items, tuning, songs, voices** are registries with plain objects (§7, §10, §11).\n379\t  Adding a persona, an enemy or an area never changes code in `src/`.\n380\t\n381\t### 5.6 Events (`S7.bus.on(type, fn)` / `emit`)\n382\t\n383\t`shot {persona, weapon, from, dir, hit}` · `hit {target, part: 'body'|'warm'|'shield'|'prop', dmg}` · `crit {enemy}` ·\n384\t`kill {enemy, type, crit}` · `explode {at, radius, dmg, source}` · `reveal {enemy, by}` · `scan {at, radius}` ·\n385\t`laugh {enemy, style, at, loud}` · `pickup {kind, amount, at}` · `heal {amount}` · `hurt {persona, dmg, from}` ·\n386\t`freeze {persona, at}` · `rescue {persona}` · `switch {from, to}` · `junction {node, options}` · `gate {gate, ok,\n387\treason}` · `skill {persona, skill}` · `area {from, to}` · `room7 {in}` · `save {slot}` · `beat {id}` ·\n388\t`boss {id, phase}` · `music {cue}` · `tutorial {id}` · `result {target, stats, rank}` · `echo {kind}`.\n389\tAudio, VFX, HUD splashes, rumble and tutorials only listen. They never change sim state.\n390\t\n391\t### 5.7 The test and tool handle: `game.debug`\n392\t\n393\t```\n394\tsnapshot() → { scene, mode, area, node, edge, s, dir, speed, persona, hp, red, rime, items, pins, edelweiss,\n395\t               flags (story vars), objective, enemies: { alive, revealed }, boss: { id, phase, hp }, aim: { yaw, pitch },\n396\t               target, results, time, hash }\n397\tteleport(area, node) · setPersona(id) · give(item, n) · addRed(n) · addRime(n) · tune(key, v) · cheat(name, on)\n398\tkillAll() · revealAll() · freeze(personaId) · wave(id) · beat(id) · boss(id, phase) · skipCutscene() · hash()\n399\tbot: { run(route), stop(), status() }\n400\t```\n401\t`hash()` is a stable hash of the sim state (walker, roster, enemies, economy, flags, rng positions). `game.stateHash`\n402\treturns it too, so the engine's recorded sessions (`?record=1`, `engine/tools/replay.mjs`) stop at the first frame\n403\twhere the game diverges.\n404\t\n405\t### 5.8 Build (`node tools/build.mjs`)\n406\t\n407\t1. Read `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`, `vendor/aihub/session.js` and the asset-store sets we use (cut\n408\t   down to the entries we use).\n409\t2. Concatenate the bundle in this order: `src/0*`–`src/8*`, `content/**` (sorted), `src/9*`. Wrap it in\n410\t   `(() => { 'use strict'; … })();`, with the namespace at `globalThis.S7`.\n411\t3. Fill the page template (§5.1). The template fills `<title>`, the version line, the build date and the engine\n412\t   versions. The boot splash markup comes from the engine itself: load `engine/my-3d2dge.js` in a vm and call\n413\t   `E.bootSplash('SKILLER7')`.\n414\t4. Write `../Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html` (the root) and `out/bundle.js` (for the Node\n415\t   tests).\n416\t5. Fail the build if the bundle contains `TODO`, `FIXME`, `TBD`, `XXX`, `lorem` or `placeholder` (case-insensitive,\n417\t   outside the engine), or if `tools/validate-content.mjs` reports an error.\n418\t\n419\t### 5.9 Planned engine changes (`// S7 PATCH:` each)\n420\t\n421\t1. **Humanoid hats** (`_hat` in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, around line 4628, next to `'cap'`, `'helmet'`, `'band'`): add\n422\t   `'beanie'` (a disc and a pompom ball: `h.pom`), `'ushanka'` (a fur disc and ear flaps), `'felt'` (a Tyrolean hat:\n423\t   brim, crown, feather `h.feather`), `'beret'` (a flattened disc tilted to one side), `'hardhat'` (a helmet with a brim\n424\t   and a headlamp glow), `'racehelmet'` (a full helmet with a visor in `h.visor`), `'pillbox'` (a bellhop cap).\n425\t2. **Humanoid accessories** (a new `acc` option drawn in the head block after the eyes, and on the torso): `goggles`\n426\t   (a band and two lenses), `visor`, `moustache`, `beard`, `scarf` (a neck ring and two tails that trail with motion),\n427\t   `shades`, `cigar` (with an ember glow), `badge`, `bandolier`, `pack`.\n428\t3. **Humanoid weapons** (in the `o.weapon` branch, beside `'gun'`): `'revolver'`, `'poles'` (two ski poles, one in\n429\t   each hand, barrels at the tips), `'rifle'` (long, with a scope), `'shotgun'`, `'alphorn'` (a long wooden horn: on the\n430\t   back in rail mode, held forward with `point: true`), `'camera'` (a box with a flash), `'launcher'` (a tube with a gas\n431\t   bottle), `'pistol'` (small).\n432\t4. **Skis** (a new option `skis: { color }`): two long lines under the feet along the facing, tips curled, drawn only\n433\t   while `rig.state.skis` is true.\n434\t5. **Default button symbols: PlayStation.** In `STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs` (around line 2999), set the default `value`\n435\t   to `'playstation'`. The option list stays (Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo, Positions).\n436\t6. **AI Hub rule:** ignore `pause` from Pad9 (Options) while Pad8 (Create) is held, only when `AIHub.active`.\n437\t\n438\tEvery rig change must draw on cards (the 3D world) and in `rig.drawPortrait` (dialogs) alike. Check each one with\n439\t`engine/tools/filmstrip.mjs` on a small test page. Then run `node engine/kits/test.mjs` (the kit pages load\n440\t`../my-3d2dge.js`, which is our patched engine) and `node engine/game/test.mjs` to prove the 2D kits and the template\n441\tstill pass. The 3D module stays unpatched unless a blocker shows up; record any change there too.\n442\t\n443\t---\n444\t\n445\t## 6. Controls and 10-foot UX\n446\t\n447\t### 6.1 The action map\n448\t\n449\tThe engine's custom input preset (codes: `KeyW`, `Mouse0` left, `Mouse1` middle, `Mouse2` right, `Pad0`…`Pad15` in\n450\tthe standard mapping). Every action can be rebound on the standard Controls screen.\n451\t\n452\t| Action (id) | DualSense (Xbox glyphs) | Keyboard and mouse | Mode |\n453\t|---|---|---|---|\n454\t| Move (`move`, hold) | ✕ (A) or left stick up | W / ↑ | rail, junction (go), ski (tuck) |\n455\t| Quick turn / hockey stop (`turn`) | ○ (B) or a flick down on the left stick | S / ↓ | rail / ski |\n456\t| Pick a branch (`left`/`right`) | left stick or d-pad ← → | A D / ← → / hover a marker (a click goes) | junction |\n457\t| Interact / check (`interact`) | △ (Y) | E or F / click the object | rail |\n458\t| Heal (`heal`, hold) | □ (X) | H | rail |\n459\t| Featherbed ring (`ring`, hold; tap = last persona) | L1 (LB) | Tab (hold; aim at a portrait with the mouse) · 1–7 pick directly | rail |\n460\t| Skill (`skill`) | R1 (RB) | Q | rail, aim |\n461\t| Aim (`aim`, hold or toggle) | L2 (LT) | right mouse | rail, ski (after stopping), lift |\n462\t| Fire (`fire`) | R2 (RT) | left mouse | aim |\n463\t| Look / aim | right stick (left stick at 60%) | mouse (look or pointer mode, §7.3) | aim |\n464\t| Scan (`scan`) | L1 (LB) | Space or middle mouse | aim (the ring is off while aiming) |\n465\t| Reload (`reload`) | □ (X) | R | aim |\n466\t| Map (`map`) | Create (View) | M | rail |\n467\t| Pause | Options (Menu) | Esc / P | any |\n468\t| Menus | d-pad or sticks, ✕ confirm, ○ back | arrows / WASD, Enter or Space, Esc or Backspace; hover focuses, click picks | menus |\n469\t\n470\t- Contextual pairs share a button: L1 is the ring in rail mode and Scan in aim mode; □ is Heal in rail mode and\n471\t  Reload in aim mode. These modes never overlap, and the Controls screen lists each action separately.\n472\t- **Aim: hold** (default) or **toggle** (setting). Leaving aim mode returns to the rail shot with no input lost.\n473\t- **Ski edges:** move = tuck, release = carve (slower), turn = hockey stop (stops on the slope; then aim works). Move\n474\t  pushes off again.\n475\t\n476\t### 6.2 Prompts and glyphs\n477\t\n478\t- Every prompt calls `game.input.label(action)`. It follows the last device used (`input.lastDevice`): ✕ ○ □ △ L1 R1\n479\t  L2 R2 on a controller (A B X Y LB RB LT RT with the Xbox setting), key names and \"Left click\", \"Right click\" on\n480\t  keyboard and mouse. With the patch in §5.9, the default is PlayStation, and Settings → Controller → Button symbols\n481\t  offers Xbox. Our Settings screen also shows a shortcut toggle, \"Controller buttons: PlayStation / Xbox\", in group\n482\t  Game; it writes the same `glyphs` setting.\n483\t- A prompt is a glyph chip (the label in a rounded box drawn with `E.ui.box`) plus a verb in capitals: `△ CHECK  Murmel`.\n484\t  Prompts change the moment the device changes.\n485\t\n486\t### 6.3 The mouse in the world and in menus\n487\t\n488\t- **Rail mode:** hovering a junction marker highlights that branch and a click takes it. Hovering an interactable's\n489\t  marker (a diamond over it) focuses it; a click interacts. Hovering a Featherbed portrait focuses it; a click picks it.\n490\t  The UI turns each into an intent (§5.2).\n491\t- **Aim mode:** see §7.3 (look mode with a captured pointer, or pointer mode like a light gun).\n492\t- **Menus:** hover moves the focus, a click activates, a drag moves a slider, the wheel scrolls lists and changes\n493\t  focused values. The engine's E.Shell screens already work this way; our canvas widget kit (`80-ui-kit.js`) copies\n494\t  the behaviour exactly.\n495\t\n496\t### 6.4 Layout rules (a TV at 10 feet)\n497\t\n498\t- `res: 'ps1'`, `expand: true`: at least 320×240 game pixels, growing on the long side (about 427×240 on 16:9). Anchor\n499\t  every HUD piece to `r.W` and `r.H`. Keep a **5% safe margin** on every edge (TV overscan).\n500\t- Minimum text: the 5×7 font at scale 1 (on a 1080p TV that is about 31 px tall). Important text (prompts, splashes,\n501\t  names) uses scale 2; title cards use `E.font.title` at scale 3–5.\n502\t- **No dense screens:** at most 7 focusable items on a screen, at most about 40 words of body text at once, one idea per\n503\t  screen. Lists longer than 7 page with L1/R1 (Q/E, or the wheel).\n504\t- Focus is always obvious: a red bar, the item scaled ×1.1, and a tick sound. Confirm and back are the same everywhere\n505\t  (✕ and ○; Enter and Esc).\n506\t- Never rely on colour alone. Warm spots are a diamond ring as well as orange. Gates show a persona's face. Damage\n507\t  directions are shapes.\n508\t\n509\t### 6.5 Touch-only devices\n510\t\n511\t- **Touch-only** means `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)')` and no `(any-pointer: fine)` and `navigator.maxTouchPoints >\n512\t  0`, and no gamepad connected, and no key pressed yet.\n513\t- Then the title shows, centred: **CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED**, a line \"Connect a controller or a keyboard to\n514\t  play\", and a big **SETTINGS** button (a tap calls `game.menu('settings')`). The corner's menu and full-screen buttons\n515\t  stay. There are **no touch controls**: every scene has `touch: []` and `game.input.touchEnabled = false`.\n516\t- The idle timer still runs: after 10 s the **attract mode** plays (§7.23). A tap stops it and returns to the notice.\n517\t- If a gamepad connects or a key is pressed, the notice is replaced by the normal title at once.\n518\t- In an AI Hub session on a touch-only device, call `AIHub.fail('Controller or keyboard required')`.\n519\t\n520\t---\n521\t\n522\t## 7. Systems\n523\t\n524\tEach system lists its rules, its numbers (**Piste** difficulty unless stated) and its tests. Speeds are world units\n525\tper second (16 = 1 m).\n526\t\n527\t### 7.1 Rails and movement (`11-rails.js`)\n528\t\n529\t**Graph.** Nodes `{ id, at, kind: junction | room7 | exit | door | stop | lift | npc }` and edges\n530\t`{ id, a, b, kind, path (E.Path, catmull), oneWay, gate, cams, tags }`. A walk edge is two-way. Ski, lift and jump"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a19d4130-5ad5-4253-b2f9-ab1e00f8ce04","uuid":"d7244a6d-2497-41b9-b0d9-03b1a7cf118f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T09:06:39.331Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-020620__x696bb3/work/PLAN.md","content":"# SKILLER7\n\n*Seven Killers, One Mountain.*\n\n**Title.** Picked the way TASK.md says: 50 playful riffs on \"Killer7\" were brainstormed silently, then one was drawn\nwith a real random number generator (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`, OS entropy): index 13, **Skiller7**. It reads three\nways at once (SKI + KILLER + SKILL), and the whole design leans on that. In file and folder names, GameTitle is\n`Skiller7` and SOURCE is `Killer7`.\n\n**Pitch.** Piz Nada is a neutral micro-state on one mountain, and it has snowed there for a hundred years. On the top\nfloor of its sanatorium, in Room 7, a 104-year-old former ski champion called Oma Holle lies under a mountain of\nfeatherbed. When she shakes it, seven killers step out of her wardrobe. The Canton's Department of Discretion sends\nthem \"guests\" to check out: agents of Mister Föhn, the warm wind that makes people laugh until they disappear. You move\non rails through a grand hotel, a ski village, a summit conference, a black ski run, a gondola and a glacier vault.\nThen you stop, look through your persona's eyes, scan for the laughing, invisible Whiteouts, and shoot their Warm Spot\nbefore they reach you.\n\n---\n\n## 0. How to use this plan\n\n- **TASK.md + PLAN.md are the whole brief.** TASK.md decides deliverables and process. This plan decides design. Where\n  this plan gives a number, use it as the default and expose the important ones as live dev sliders (§7.22).\n- **Everything in the game is original.** We take Killer7's *structure* (rail movement, stop-and-aim first-person\n  scanning, weak-point instant kills, one body with several killer personas, a save room, a blood economy, cryptic\n  hint-givers, chapters called targets, bosses with odd rules) and its *tone*. We do not use its names, characters,\n  art, music, levels or look.\n- **Build in this order:** a playable greybox golden path first, then systems to full depth, then content, then polish\n  (§12). The scope is sized to be built whole. TASK.md forbids TODOs, placeholders and stubs, so build every system and\n  piece of content in this plan.\n- **Mark every engine change** in the engine files with `// S7 PATCH:` and a reason, and keep a list for the\n  hand-back's Engine Feedback (§16).\n\n---\n\n## 1. At a glance\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Title | **SKILLER7** (shown with that spelling; `Skiller7` in file names) |\n| Tagline | Seven Killers, One Mountain |\n| Source | Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005) |\n| Genre | On-rails action-adventure shooter: rail movement, first-person scan-and-shoot, persona-switch puzzles, boss fights. AI Hub catalogue genre: **Shooter** |\n| Perspective | Third-person cinematic rail cameras (fixed, dolly, follow and crane shots that hard-cut) while moving; **first person** while aiming; a schematic top-down map in the pause menu |\n| Players | 1 in the campaign. 1–4 in **Echo Run** (AI Hub ghost mode; solo, you race your own recorded echo) |\n| Length | about 45–60 min: Target 00 \"Turn-Down Service\" (about 12 min), Target 01 \"Après\" (35–45 min), epilogue. Echo Run rounds take about 2 min |\n| Engine | my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** handoff (the 2D engine runs the game) plus the engine's own **3D module v0.85.2** (three.js r182, Rapier 0.19.3 from a CDN) |\n| Look | The engine's native style: PS1-resolution picture (`'pixels'`), characters as pixel-art rigs drawn on cards, the 3D module's **Comic cel** filter (flat bands of light, ink lines). Stark white snow, black shadows, saturated red |\n| Animation | The engine's procedural rigs (`E.Humanoid`, `E.Blob`, `E.MOVES`, poses), extended with new hats, accessories, weapons, skis and poses; first-person weapon models with the overlay pen |\n| Audio | All in code: engine chip-synth songs, engine SFX voices, our own WebAudio laughter synth and Animalese-style gibberish voices |\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, `ghost` mode (Echo Run). On its own, the game runs without the hub |\n| Ship | `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html` and `exit-interview.json` in this folder. Dev files go in `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Killer7 → Skiller7: what we keep, what is ours\n\n| What makes Killer7 distinctive | Skiller7 (original) |\n|---|---|\n| Hold one button to run along fixed rails; junction icons; a quick 180° turn | Rails through hotel halls and village lanes; **downhill ski rails** with momentum and hockey stops; **lifts** (T-bar, gondola) you can shoot from; **ski jumps**; an **avalanche chase** |\n| Stop, aim in first person, scan to reveal invisible laughing suicide bombers, weak spots that kill at once | **Whiteouts**: Föhn-touched people who laugh themselves invisible. You hear them, see their **footprints in the snow and their breath**, and a **Scan** pulse reveals them. Shoot the glowing **Warm Spot** and they thaw on the spot (\"THAW.\") |\n| One body, seven personas, each with a gun and a field ability | **Oma Holle's Seven**: a ski patrolman, a downhill racer, a telemark aristocrat, a fur-coated tycoon, a yodeler, a press photographer and an avalanche engineer |\n| A cleaner who retrieves fallen personas | **The Sweep** skis in from Room 7 and chips frozen personas out of the ice |\n| A save room with a maid and a TV | **Room 7**: Sister Rosalind, the **Cuckoo Clock** (save), the **Wax Bench** (upgrades: you wax your skills), the **Wardrobe** of seven coats (persona select) |\n| Thin blood heals, thick blood buys upgrades | **Red** (shared; heals and fuels skills) and **Rime** (upgrade crystals) |\n| A cryptic ghost and a grotesque hint-giver | **Pim the Lift Boy** (the ghost of a 1926 lift attendant on a floating chairlift seat) and **Murmel** (a resort-mascot marmot bungeed to a ski rack: \"Gratuity is customary.\") |\n| A handler on the phone, political allegory, an eternal game between two gods | **The Concierge** (hotel euphemisms for murder), the **Thaw Accord**, Oma Holle against **Mister Föhn** over a chessboard of ice |\n| Cel shading, flat colour, heavy black shadows, huge type | Comic-cel filter and ink. White-on-white snow made readable by ink outlines. Red on snow. Huge title cards |\n| Chapters called targets, each ending in a strange boss | **Target 00** (the Bellcaptain in an elevator shaft). **Target 01** (an oompah band you shoot on the beat; the Powder King: a laughing snowman colossus, a slalom duel, and an interview on a stuck chairlift) |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Deliverables, folders, ground rules\n\n**At the end, this folder holds:** TASK.md, STAGE.md (if present), PLAN.md, the built game\n`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html`, `exit-interview.json` beside it, and the dev folder\n`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` with every source, tool and test. If your TASK.md gives the\nnames with a different model or date, use your TASK.md's names (GameTitle `Skiller7`, SOURCE `Killer7`).\n\n**Shared machine rules (from TASK.md):**\n- Read only the files you need. Other agents share this directory, the machine's ports and its processes.\n- Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. Only the AI Hub harness needs a server. Start it with\n  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (it prints the port), check that the served page really is your game\n  (its `<title>` starts with \"Skiller7\"), and stop it by its own PID. Never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever\n  holds a port.\n- `/tmp` on this machine is a small tmpfs and may be full. Keep large files (vendor copies, screenshots, logs) inside\n  the dev folder, and run Node and Playwright with `TMPDIR` pointing at a `tmp/` folder inside it (Chromium's\n  profiles go there). Playwright's Chromium 1243 is already installed in `~/.cache/ms-playwright`.\n- Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n- One test command prints only failures and a one-line count (§14). Rerun it only after changing code it covers. Use\n  screenshots only for what a script can't check.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tech stack and pinned sources\n\n**Pinned commits** (the repositories move daily; pin them for reproducibility):\n- my-3d2dge: `4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72` (engine v0.85.2). Raw files come from\n  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72/<path>`\n- ai-hub: `f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e` (session client 1.4.0). Raw files come from\n  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e/<path>`\n\n`tools/fetch-deps.mjs` downloads these once, idempotently:\n\n| What | Repo path | Dev-folder path | Shipped? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **The whole handoff folder**, layout kept so its tools, kits and tests run unchanged: the 2D engine (full, readable, every module: story, rpg, songs, arcade…), `modules/`, `kits/`, `game/`, `tools/`, `docs/`, `src/assets/` (the store), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `package.json` | `dist/handoff/` (a sparse checkout at the pinned commit, or the GitHub tree API plus raw downloads) | `engine/` | only `engine/my-3d2dge.js` is inlined |\n| Asset store sets we use | `dist/handoff/src/assets/sets/{kenney-holiday,kenney-furniture,kaykit-furniture-bits,kenney-nature}.js` | `engine/src/assets/sets/` (part of the copy above) | inlined: only the entries we use, cut with `engine/tools/asset-set.mjs` |\n| 3D module (ES module, header = its manual) | `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | inlined as a module |\n| 3D reference slices (read, don't ship) | `src/starter3d/00-shell.js`, `30-shooter.js`, `40-horror.js`, `60-flight.js` | `ref/` | no |\n| three.js r182 (tests only) | `vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js`, `three.core.min.js`, `three.tsl.min.js` | `vendor/three-0.182.0/` | no (CDN) |\n| Rapier 0.19.3 (tests only) | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js` | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/` | no (CDN) |\n| AI Hub client | ai-hub `lib/hub/session.js` | `vendor/aihub/session.js` | inlined **unmodified**, first script in `<head>` |\n| AI Hub harness (tests only) | ai-hub `lib/hub/harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` | `vendor/aihub/` | no |\n\n**Runtime import map (shipped).** All four URLs were checked: HTTP 200, `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`.\n```json\n{ \"imports\": {\n  \"three\":        \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/tsl\":    \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.min.js\",\n  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs\" } }\n```\n`three.webgpu.min.js` imports `./three.core.min.js` beside it on the CDN. Rapier's compat build embeds its wasm, so\nnothing else is fetched. Inline module scripts and import maps work from `file://`, so the single file plays when\ndouble-clicked (online). The AI Hub localizes these engine URLs when it ingests a game (its shelf has three 0.180–0.186.1\nand Rapier 0.19.3). Tests route the URLs to the `vendor/` copies, so they never depend on the network.\n\n**Tooling:** Node 24 (on the machine), Python 3 (the harness server only), Playwright **1.63.0** (pinned as in the\nhandoff's package.json) with `npx playwright install chromium`. No other libraries, no bundler: the build script\nconcatenates (§5.8).\n\n**Verified while planning** (a throwaway spike, not part of the build): one HTML file holding the 2D engine (classic\nscript), the 3D module (inline module) and this import map, opened from `file://` in headless Chromium 1243 through\nPlaywright 1.63.0, loaded three r182 and Rapier from jsdelivr and booted `E.World3D` with no page errors. Headless\nChromium has no WebGPU, so both `?backend=webgl` and the default ran on WebGL 2. A level with `ground: 'snow'`, a\nHumanoid on a card with `pose: false` that our code `place()`d and `rig.update()`d every step, a `'fixed'` camera set in\n`draw`, `world.glow` (it already reads as a Warm Spot), `world.project` and the `cel` filter all rendered as expected.\nTwo lessons: an actor's position reads back from Rapier in float32 (about 3e-6 off), so the sim keeps its own positions\nand never reads actors; and `'fixed'` cameras don't cut walls, so every shot must sit in open space (§7.2).\n\n**Why the 3D module.** Killer7 is cinematic third-person rails plus first-person aiming. The handoff's 2D views can't do\nfirst person. The engine's own 3D module (the same engine and release, documented as its 3D side) gives exactly what\nwe need: `'fixed'` cameras we pose ourselves, a first-person overlay for the gun, `world.project` for the HUD, fog,\nlights, shadows, terrain for ski runs, the 2D engine's rigs drawn as cards, and a built-in **Comic cel** filter. The 2D\nengine still runs everything else: scenes, the fixed-step clock, input and rebinding, the pause menu and settings,\nsaves, the Story and Dialog modules, the music clock, the HUD font and widgets.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Architecture\n\n### 5.1 The built page, in order\n\n```html\n<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<script>/*! aihub-session 1.4.0 … pasted unmodified … */</script>       <!-- first script, right after charset -->\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n<title>Skiller7 — Seven Killers, One Mountain</title>\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Seven killers share one body and one mountain. Ride the rails, stop, scan for the laughing invisible Whiteouts and shoot the warm spot.\">\n<meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#d81e2a\">\n<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"data:image/svg+xml,…a red 7 on white…\">\n<meta name=\"hub:source\" content=\"Killer7\">\n<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1–4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Fastest down the Black Run wins; crits cut time\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Hold W/✕ ski · S/○ stop · RMB/L2 aim · LMB/R2 shoot · Space/L1 scan\">\n<script type=\"importmap\">{ …§4… }</script>\n<style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#000;overflow:hidden} #stage{position:relative;width:100%;height:100%}\n#view{position:absolute;left:0;top:0;pointer-events:none;z-index:0} #screen{position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;image-rendering:pixelated;z-index:1;background:transparent}</style>\n</head><body>\n<!-- E.bootSplash('SKILLER7') markup: the splash shows while scripts load -->\n<div id=\"stage\"><canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas></div>\n<script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js v0.85.2 (with S7 PATCHes) */</script>\n<script>/* the Skiller7 bundle: src + content, classic script, defines globalThis.S7, starts nothing */</script>\n<script type=\"module\">/* engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js v0.85.2: imports three and Rapier through the import map */</script>\n<script type=\"module\">S7.start({ World3D: My3D2dge.World3D });</script>\n<script>/* watchdog: if S7.started is still false after 20 s, show \"The 3D libraries didn't load. Check the connection and reload.\" with a Reload button; in a session, call AIHub.fail(that message) */</script>\n</body></html>\n```\nThe build escapes every `</script` inside inlined code as `<\\/script`.\n\n### 5.2 Sim and presenter\n\n```\n            game.input ──► Intent layer ◄── Bot (attract, tests)\n                               │ intents (move, turn, branch, aim yaw/pitch deltas, fire, scan, skill, interact…)\n                               ▼\n  ┌──────────────── SIM (deterministic, fixed step 1/120 s, runs in Node) ────────────────┐\n  │ rails · walker · camera director · personas · weapons · skills · whiteouts · combat  │\n  │ scan · bosses · economy · items · gates · story/beats · saves · results · echo run    │\n  └──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────┘\n                     state (read only)                   events (S7.bus)\n                                 ▼                              ▼\n        Presenter3D (browser) / NullPresenter (Node)      Audio · UI widgets · HUD\n        World3D worlds, actors, rigs, pen, glows,         (headless-safe; audio no-ops\n        lights, FX, first-person overlay, camera          without an AudioContext)\n```\n- **The sim** owns all game state. It uses only the 2D engine (`E.Path`, `E.rng`, `E.Story`, `E.Dialog`, `E.Menu`,\n  `game.input`, `game.audio.pos`, `game.saves`, `game.script`, `game.tween`) and our own math. It never touches three.js,\n  the DOM or `E.World3D`, and it never reads anything back from the presenter.\n- **The presenter** turns state into pictures. It has two hooks: `step(dt)` in the play scene's update after\n  `sim.update` (mirror actors, `rig.update`, `world.step`) and `draw(r)` (pen, glows, lights, `world.draw(r)`, then the\n  HUD). **NullPresenter** has the same interface and does nothing; Node tests use it.\n- **Mouse picking goes through intents.** The UI projects world anchors with `world.project` and turns a hover or a\n  click into an intent (`branch = 2`, `interact = 'murmel'`). The sim also keeps its own simple projection (from the\n  camera director's pose) for stick-to-branch mapping, so headless play needs no presenter.\n- Level geometry exists twice from one source: the presenter builds `World3D` from the area's rows or terrain\n  function, and the sim builds a `LevelGrid` from the same data for line of sight and paths.\n\n### 5.3 Source tree (dev folder)\n\n```\nSkiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/\n  package.json            { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { playwright: \"1.63.0\" },\n                            scripts: { build: \"node tools/build.mjs\", test: \"node tests/run.mjs\" } }\n  engine/                 a copy of dist/handoff/ (my-3d2dge.js patched; kits/, tools/, src/assets/…) + my-3d2dge-3d.js (§4)\n  vendor/                 three-0.182.0/, rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/, aihub/               (§4)\n  ref/                    3D starter slices, for reading\n  src/\n    00-core.js        S7 namespace, VERSION, S7.bus, registries (S7.area/persona/weapon/enemy/item/beat/song/voice),\n                      named E.rng streams ('spawn','spread','laugh','fx'), units (M = 16 world units = 1 m)\n    01-math.js        vectors, ray–sphere, ray–capsule, segment distance, angles, easing helpers\n    02-tuning.js      TUNING table {key: {def, min, max, step, label, group}}, live values, reset, difficulty multipliers\n    05-input.js       the S7 input preset, intent layer (human and bot), last-device tracking, idle timer,\n                      touch-only detection, mouse modes (look / pointer), stick flick detection\n    10-levelgrid.js   LevelGrid from level rows and legend (floor and wall heights, pits, stairs, platforms),\n                      terrain sampler, line of sight (tile DDA with heights / terrain ray-march), BFS flow fields\n    11-rails.js       RailGraph (nodes, edges as E.Path), Walker (walk, ski, lift, jump, stairs), junctions,\n                      gate checks, spot proximity\n    12-camera.js      CameraDirector: shots, cuts, blends, aim transition, overrides, shake; sim-side projection\n    20-personas.js    roster state, HP, stats, availability, switching, frozen/fallen\n    21-weapons.js     fire cycles, magazines, reloads, spread, recoil, hitscan/cone/lob kinds, effective range\n    22-skills.js      flare, tuck, longlook, makeitrain + buyout, echo, flash, charge, rescue\n    30-whiteouts.js   enemy types, behaviours, warm spots, tells (footprints, breath), laugh scheduling,\n                      spawner, waves\n    31-combat.js      damage pipeline; hits on enemies, props and targets; crits; explosions; projectiles\n                      (snowballs, suitcases, medals, shells); drops and pickup magnet\n    32-scan.js        scan pulse, reveal timers, laugh-direction cues\n    33-bosses.js      boss controllers: grau, jollyfour, hansi (with phases)\n    40-economy.js     Red, Rime, healing, Wax Bench costs and effects\n    41-items.js       inventory, key items, collectibles, Dossier entries\n    42-gates.js       gate kinds: rope, jump, farbell, buyout, echowall, crystal, exposure, codelock, cornice,\n                      serac, icelock, edelweiss, key\n    50-story.js       E.Story setup, people (speaker ↔ rig ↔ voice), flags, objectives, beats (game.script),\n                      title cards, letterbox\n    51-npcs.js        Murmel hints, Pim appearances, phones, frozen tableaux\n    52-room7.js       entering and leaving, the clock, the Wax Bench, the Wardrobe, the bed, the telephone\n    60-save.js        serialize/deserialize, game.saves wiring, autosave, versions and migrate\n    61-results.js     per-target stats and ranks\n    70-audio.js       buses, song table, SFX definitions, positional helper, music state (layers, cues), ambience\n    71-voice.js       Animalese voice synth and its dialog hookup\n    72-laugh.js       laughter synth (styles) and positional emitters\n    80-ui-kit.js      canvas widget kit: focus lists, buttons, sliders, toggles, tabs, grids, glyph prompts;\n                      pad, keys and mouse (hover focuses, click activates, drag moves sliders)\n    81-hud.js         the in-play HUD\n    82-screens.js     title, Featherbed ring, junction markers, Wax Bench, Wardrobe, map, Dossier and items,\n                      code lock, target cards, results, game over, About/Credits, touch-only notice,\n                      attract overlay\n    83-shell.js       a themed E.Shell (pause, settings, controls, help, saves) and its pause items\n    84-dev.js         dev mode panel and dev overlays\n    85-bot.js         the bot: route runner, rail navigation, combat (used by attract mode and tests)\n    86-attract.js     attract director: vignettes, cheats, any-input exit\n    87-hub.js         AI Hub session flow, Echo Run networking, solo echo recording\n    88-echorun.js     Echo Run rules: course, timing, penalties, results\n    90-present3d.js   Presenter3D: a world per area, actor mirroring, rigs, accessories, pen drawing,\n                      glows, lights, camera\n    91-fx3d.js        snowfall, footprint and red-snow decals, breath, scan ring, splashes, flurry transition\n    92-firstperson.js weapon models and their animations on the overlay pen\n    93-present-null.js NullPresenter\n    95-scenes.js      scenes: title, attract, play (Room 7 is a mode of play), results, credits, gameover,\n                      echorun, hub\n    99-start.js       S7.start(opts): new E.Game, boot steps, scenes, game.debug\n  content/\n    tuning.js personas.js weapons.js enemies.js items.js shapes.js things3d.js songs.js voices.js\n    attract.js echorun.js\n    areas/  room7.js 00A-lobby.js 00B-thirdfloor.js 01A-village.js 01B-palais.js 01C-blackrun.js\n            01D-gondola.js 01E-archive.js 01F-summit.js\n    story/  common.js t00.js t01.js epilogue.js barks.js\n  tools/   build.mjs  validate-content.mjs  fetch-deps.mjs\n  tests/   run.mjs  lib/node-harness.mjs  lib/browser-harness.mjs  node/*.test.mjs  browser/*.test.mjs\n           routes/golden.js (the playthrough's route)  out/ (screenshots, logs, the hub copy; never shipped)\n```\n`content/things3d.js` only defines `S7.things3d = W3 => { W3.THINGS.chandelier = (pen, t, time) => …; … }`, and\nPresenter3D calls it in its init, so nothing there runs in Node. Everything else in `content/` is plain data and runs\nanywhere.\n\n### 5.4 The play loop and the mode machine\n\nThe play scene (`pausable: true`, `touch: []`):\n```\nupdate(dt):  if (ui.modal) { ui.modal.update(dt); return; }   // dev panel, Wax Bench, map… freeze the sim\n             intents = Intent.read(game.input | bot)            // one place turns devices into intents\n             sim.update(dt, intents)                            // everything in §7, in a fixed order\n             presenter.step(dt)                                 // mirror, rigs, world.step (browser only)\ndraw(r):     presenter.draw(r)                                  // world.draw, then the HUD and screens\n```\n`sim.update` order: modes → walker → camera director → skills → weapons and projectiles → whiteouts → scan and\nreveals → combat resolution → pickups → economy → bosses → triggers and waves → story and beats → results clock →\nemit events.\n\n**Modes** (one at a time; `S7.sim.mode`):\n\n| Mode | Enter | Exit | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `rail` | default | — | move, turn, interact, heal, skill, ring, aim |\n| `junction` | arrive at a node with 3+ edges | a branch is chosen and move is held | the walker is stopped; branch markers show |\n| `aim` | aim held (or toggled) in rail/lift mode, or on a ski edge after a hockey stop | aim released | first person; the walker is stopped except on lifts and `aimOnTheMove` edges |\n| `ski` | on a `ski` edge | leave the edge | automatic downhill movement; move = tuck, turn = hockey stop |\n| `lift` | board a lift node | the end node | automatic movement; aiming allowed |\n| `talk` | interact with an NPC, phone or tableau | the dialog ends | sim frozen except ambience |\n| `ring` | hold ring | release (switch) | time × 0.15 |\n| `switching` | a persona is chosen | 0.45 s later | invulnerable |\n| `cutscene` | a beat script takes the camera | the script ends | skippable with start/pause → \"Skip scene?\" |\n| `frozen` | the active persona's HP reaches 0 | 1.2 s later → `rescue` | the freeze animation |\n| `rescue` | after `frozen` | the statue is chipped free | you play the Sweep |\n| `gameover` | the Sweep's HP reaches 0 | Continue or Quit | |\n| `modal` | a canvas screen opens | it closes | the sim is frozen |\n\n### 5.5 Data formats (the designer's tools)\n\nAll content is data in `content/`, checked by `tools/validate-content.mjs` (§14.7). **Units in area data:** x and y\nin **tiles** (1 tile = 1 m = 16 world units; row 0 is north, column 0 is west), z in **metres**. The loader multiplies\nby 16. Use `.5` for tile centres.\n\n```js\nS7.area('00A', {\n  title: 'GRAND HOTEL NADA', sub: 'LOBBY', target: 0, music: 'hotel', ambience: 'roomtone',\n  world: {                                   // given to new E.World3D(game, …) and parsed by the sim's LevelGrid\n    level: { rows: [/* 28 strings of 40 chars */], legend: { /* World3D legend: floor, wall, pillar, stairs,\n             platform, pit, torch, mark, shape */ }, ground: 'tiles', colors: { a: '#e8e4dc', b: '#2a2226', size: 2 },\n             side: 'block', color: '#3a2a2e' },\n    // or terrain: { size, height: (x, y) => z /* world units, pure and shared with the sim */, color, rock, far }\n    sky: '#05040a', fog: { color: '#0a0608', near: 40, far: 420 }, ambient: ['#3a2a3a', '#120a0e', 1.2],\n    torchLights: 4, torchShadows: 1, lights: 6, glowLights: 6, overlay: { fov: 62 }, look: 'card',\n    filter: { bands: 3, ink: 1, inkW: 1.5, punch: 1.4 },  // no `look` and no `res` here: options given to a new world\n    camera: { mode: 'fixed', near: 1 }                    // override the player's 3D settings, so they come from §7.21\n  },\n  nodes: {                                   // t: [x, y, z?]; z defaults to the floor or terrain height there\n    r7: { t: [33.5, 20.5], kind: 'room7' },  j1: { t: [28.5, 20.5], kind: 'junction' },\n    x1: { t: [20.5, 2.5, 6], kind: 'exit', label: 'STAIRS', to: ['00B', 'x1'], needs: { item: 'servicekey' } }\n  },\n  edges: [\n    { id: 'r7-j1', a: 'r7', b: 'j1', kind: 'walk',  // walk | ski | lift | jump | stairs; via: [[x, y, z?], …] bends\n      via: [[31, 20.8]], cams: [{ s: [0, 1], shot: 'fixed', at: [36, 24, 3.2], look: 'hero', fov: 50 }] },\n    { id: 'j3-g1', a: 'j3', b: 'g1', kind: 'walk', gate: 'rope', cams: [ … ] }\n  ],\n  gates:    { rope: { kind: 'buyout', price: 20, who: 'rurik', label: 'MEMBERS ONLY' } },\n  spots:    [ { id: 'murmel', node: 's1', kind: 'npc', who: 'murmel', talk: 'murmel_00A' },\n              { id: 'pin1', edge: 'j2-j4', s: .7, kind: 'pickup', item: 'pin1' },\n              { id: 'phone_bar', node: 'b1', kind: 'phone', talk: 'concierge_bar' } ],\n  spawns:   { e1: [12, 6], e2: [14, 6] },        // tile coordinates of spawn points\n  waves:    { lobby1: { spawn: [['giggler', 'e1', 0], ['giggler', 'e2', 1.5]], until: 'clear' } },\n  triggers: [ { id: 'tut_aim', edge: 'j1-j2', s: .6, once: true, beat: 't00_first_whiteout' },\n              { id: 'w_elev', node: 'j4', once: true, wave: 'lobby1' } ],\n  props:    [ ['shape', 'reception-desk', 31, 18, 0, { facing: Math.PI }], ['thing', 'chandelier', 20, 14, 5.6],\n              ['light', 20, 14, 5.2, { color: '#ffd8a0', reach: 18, power: 1.2, flicker: .05 }] ],\n  blocks:   [ [x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1] ]          // extra line-of-sight blockers (in tiles/metres): desks, cabins\n});\n```\n- **Camera shots** (per edge, in `cams`; `rcams` when walking b→a, else the same list). Every shot has `s: [s0, s1]`, its\n  share of the edge's length; shots must cover [0, 1].\n  - `fixed`: `at`, `look` ('hero' | [x, y, z]), `lookUp` (metres above the feet, default 1.1), `fov`.\n  - `dolly`: `path` (its own points), the camera slides along it as the hero moves through s0..s1; `look`, `fov`.\n  - `follow`: `back`, `side`, `up`, `ahead` (metres), `fov`, `lag` (s). The validator's default for an uncovered\n    range is `follow { back: 3.6, side: 1.1, up: 1.9, ahead: 2.5, fov: 55, lag: .12 }`.\n  - `crane`: `at`, `zFrom`, `zTo` (metres over s0..s1), `look`, `fov`.\n  - `pov`: forced first person (a dramatic corridor).\n  - `blend`: seconds of ease between this shot and the last; the default is 0, a hard cut (the Killer7 way).\n- **Beats** (`S7.beat(id, function* (G) { … })`) are `game.script` generators: dialogs (`yield G.say(node)`),\n  camera overrides (`yield G.cam(shot, seconds)`), waits, spawns, flags, cards (`yield G.card('TARGET 00', …)`). The\n  story's nodes and every line of text live in `content/story/*.js` as `E.Story` nodes.\n- **Personas, weapons, enemies, items, tuning, songs, voices** are registries with plain objects (§7, §10, §11).\n  Adding a persona, an enemy or an area never changes code in `src/`.\n\n### 5.6 Events (`S7.bus.on(type, fn)` / `emit`)\n\n`shot {persona, weapon, from, dir, hit}` · `hit {target, part: 'body'|'warm'|'shield'|'prop', dmg}` · `crit {enemy}` ·\n`kill {enemy, type, crit}` · `explode {at, radius, dmg, source}` · `reveal {enemy, by}` · `scan {at, radius}` ·\n`laugh {enemy, style, at, loud}` · `pickup {kind, amount, at}` · `heal {amount}` · `hurt {persona, dmg, from}` ·\n`freeze {persona, at}` · `rescue {persona}` · `switch {from, to}` · `junction {node, options}` · `gate {gate, ok,\nreason}` · `skill {persona, skill}` · `area {from, to}` · `room7 {in}` · `save {slot}` · `beat {id}` ·\n`boss {id, phase}` · `music {cue}` · `tutorial {id}` · `result {target, stats, rank}` · `echo {kind}`.\nAudio, VFX, HUD splashes, rumble and tutorials only listen. They never change sim state.\n\n### 5.7 The test and tool handle: `game.debug`\n\n```\nsnapshot() → { scene, mode, area, node, edge, s, dir, speed, persona, hp, red, rime, items, pins, edelweiss,\n               flags (story vars), objective, enemies: { alive, revealed }, boss: { id, phase, hp }, aim: { yaw, pitch },\n               target, results, time, hash }\nteleport(area, node) · setPersona(id) · give(item, n) · addRed(n) · addRime(n) · tune(key, v) · cheat(name, on)\nkillAll() · revealAll() · freeze(personaId) · wave(id) · beat(id) · boss(id, phase) · skipCutscene() · hash()\nbot: { run(route), stop(), status() }\n```\n`hash()` is a stable hash of the sim state (walker, roster, enemies, economy, flags, rng positions). `game.stateHash`\nreturns it too, so the engine's recorded sessions (`?record=1`, `engine/tools/replay.mjs`) stop at the first frame\nwhere the game diverges.\n\n### 5.8 Build (`node tools/build.mjs`)\n\n1. Read `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`, `vendor/aihub/session.js` and the asset-store sets we use (cut\n   down to the entries we use).\n2. Concatenate the bundle in this order: `src/0*`–`src/8*`, `content/**` (sorted), `src/9*`. Wrap it in\n   `(() => { 'use strict'; … })();`, with the namespace at `globalThis.S7`.\n3. Fill the page template (§5.1). The template fills `<title>`, the version line, the build date and the engine\n   versions. The boot splash markup comes from the engine itself: load `engine/my-3d2dge.js` in a vm and call\n   `E.bootSplash('SKILLER7')`.\n4. Write `../Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html` (the root) and `out/bundle.js` (for the Node\n   tests).\n5. Fail the build if the bundle contains `TODO`, `FIXME`, `TBD`, `XXX`, `lorem` or `placeholder` (case-insensitive,\n   outside the engine), or if `tools/validate-content.mjs` reports an error.\n\n### 5.9 Planned engine changes (`// S7 PATCH:` each)\n\n1. **Humanoid hats** (`_hat` in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, around line 4628, next to `'cap'`, `'helmet'`, `'band'`): add\n   `'beanie'` (a disc and a pompom ball: `h.pom`), `'ushanka'` (a fur disc and ear flaps), `'felt'` (a Tyrolean hat:\n   brim, crown, feather `h.feather`), `'beret'` (a flattened disc tilted to one side), `'hardhat'` (a helmet with a brim\n   and a headlamp glow), `'racehelmet'` (a full helmet with a visor in `h.visor`), `'pillbox'` (a bellhop cap).\n2. **Humanoid accessories** (a new `acc` option drawn in the head block after the eyes, and on the torso): `goggles`\n   (a band and two lenses), `visor`, `moustache`, `beard`, `scarf` (a neck ring and two tails that trail with motion),\n   `shades`, `cigar` (with an ember glow), `badge`, `bandolier`, `pack`.\n3. **Humanoid weapons** (in the `o.weapon` branch, beside `'gun'`): `'revolver'`, `'poles'` (two ski poles, one in\n   each hand, barrels at the tips), `'rifle'` (long, with a scope), `'shotgun'`, `'alphorn'` (a long wooden horn: on the\n   back in rail mode, held forward with `point: true`), `'camera'` (a box with a flash), `'launcher'` (a tube with a gas\n   bottle), `'pistol'` (small).\n4. **Skis** (a new option `skis: { color }`): two long lines under the feet along the facing, tips curled, drawn only\n   while `rig.state.skis` is true.\n5. **Default button symbols: PlayStation.** In `STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs` (around line 2999), set the default `value`\n   to `'playstation'`. The option list stays (Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo, Positions).\n6. **AI Hub rule:** ignore `pause` from Pad9 (Options) while Pad8 (Create) is held, only when `AIHub.active`.\n\nEvery rig change must draw on cards (the 3D world) and in `rig.drawPortrait` (dialogs) alike. Check each one with\n`engine/tools/filmstrip.mjs` on a small test page. Then run `node engine/kits/test.mjs` (the kit pages load\n`../my-3d2dge.js`, which is our patched engine) and `node engine/game/test.mjs` to prove the 2D kits and the template\nstill pass. The 3D module stays unpatched unless a blocker shows up; record any change there too.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Controls and 10-foot UX\n\n### 6.1 The action map\n\nThe engine's custom input preset (codes: `KeyW`, `Mouse0` left, `Mouse1` middle, `Mouse2` right, `Pad0`…`Pad15` in\nthe standard mapping). Every action can be rebound on the standard Controls screen.\n\n| Action (id) | DualSense (Xbox glyphs) | Keyboard and mouse | Mode |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Move (`move`, hold) | ✕ (A) or left stick up | W / ↑ | rail, junction (go), ski (tuck) |\n| Quick turn / hockey stop (`turn`) | ○ (B) or a flick down on the left stick | S / ↓ | rail / ski |\n| Pick a branch (`left`/`right`) | left stick or d-pad ← → | A D / ← → / hover a marker (a click goes) | junction |\n| Interact / check (`interact`) | △ (Y) | E or F / click the object | rail |\n| Heal (`heal`, hold) | □ (X) | H | rail |\n| Featherbed ring (`ring`, hold; tap = last persona) | L1 (LB) | Tab (hold; aim at a portrait with the mouse) · 1–7 pick directly | rail |\n| Skill (`skill`) | R1 (RB) | Q | rail, aim |\n| Aim (`aim`, hold or toggle) | L2 (LT) | right mouse | rail, ski (after stopping), lift |\n| Fire (`fire`) | R2 (RT) | left mouse | aim |\n| Look / aim | right stick (left stick at 60%) | mouse (look or pointer mode, §7.3) | aim |\n| Scan (`scan`) | L1 (LB) | Space or middle mouse | aim (the ring is off while aiming) |\n| Reload (`reload`) | □ (X) | R | aim |\n| Map (`map`) | Create (View) | M | rail |\n| Pause | Options (Menu) | Esc / P | any |\n| Menus | d-pad or sticks, ✕ confirm, ○ back | arrows / WASD, Enter or Space, Esc or Backspace; hover focuses, click picks | menus |\n\n- Contextual pairs share a button: L1 is the ring in rail mode and Scan in aim mode; □ is Heal in rail mode and\n  Reload in aim mode. These modes never overlap, and the Controls screen lists each action separately.\n- **Aim: hold** (default) or **toggle** (setting). Leaving aim mode returns to the rail shot with no input lost.\n- **Ski edges:** move = tuck, release = carve (slower), turn = hockey stop (stops on the slope; then aim works). Move\n  pushes off again.\n\n### 6.2 Prompts and glyphs\n\n- Every prompt calls `game.input.label(action)`. It follows the last device used (`input.lastDevice`): ✕ ○ □ △ L1 R1\n  L2 R2 on a controller (A B X Y LB RB LT RT with the Xbox setting), key names and \"Left click\", \"Right click\" on\n  keyboard and mouse. With the patch in §5.9, the default is PlayStation, and Settings → Controller → Button symbols\n  offers Xbox. Our Settings screen also shows a shortcut toggle, \"Controller buttons: PlayStation / Xbox\", in group\n  Game; it writes the same `glyphs` setting.\n- A prompt is a glyph chip (the label in a rounded box drawn with `E.ui.box`) plus a verb in capitals: `△ CHECK  Murmel`.\n  Prompts change the moment the device changes.\n\n### 6.3 The mouse in the world and in menus\n\n- **Rail mode:** hovering a junction marker highlights that branch and a click takes it. Hovering an interactable's\n  marker (a diamond over it) focuses it; a click interacts. Hovering a Featherbed portrait focuses it; a click picks it.\n  The UI turns each into an intent (§5.2).\n- **Aim mode:** see §7.3 (look mode with a captured pointer, or pointer mode like a light gun).\n- **Menus:** hover moves the focus, a click activates, a drag moves a slider, the wheel scrolls lists and changes\n  focused values. The engine's E.Shell screens already work this way; our canvas widget kit (`80-ui-kit.js`) copies\n  the behaviour exactly.\n\n### 6.4 Layout rules (a TV at 10 feet)\n\n- `res: 'ps1'`, `expand: true`: at least 320×240 game pixels, growing on the long side (about 427×240 on 16:9). Anchor\n  every HUD piece to `r.W` and `r.H`. Keep a **5% safe margin** on every edge (TV overscan).\n- Minimum text: the 5×7 font at scale 1 (on a 1080p TV that is about 31 px tall). Important text (prompts, splashes,\n  names) uses scale 2; title cards use `E.font.title` at scale 3–5.\n- **No dense screens:** at most 7 focusable items on a screen, at most about 40 words of body text at once, one idea per\n  screen. Lists longer than 7 page with L1/R1 (Q/E, or the wheel).\n- Focus is always obvious: a red bar, the item scaled ×1.1, and a tick sound. Confirm and back are the same everywhere\n  (✕ and ○; Enter and Esc).\n- Never rely on colour alone. Warm spots are a diamond ring as well as orange. Gates show a persona's face. Damage\n  directions are shapes.\n\n### 6.5 Touch-only devices\n\n- **Touch-only** means `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)')` and no `(any-pointer: fine)` and `navigator.maxTouchPoints >\n  0`, and no gamepad connected, and no key pressed yet.\n- Then the title shows, centred: **CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED**, a line \"Connect a controller or a keyboard to\n  play\", and a big **SETTINGS** button (a tap calls `game.menu('settings')`). The corner's menu and full-screen buttons\n  stay. There are **no touch controls**: every scene has `touch: []` and `game.input.touchEnabled = false`.\n- The idle timer still runs: after 10 s the **attract mode** plays (§7.23). A tap stops it and returns to the notice.\n- If a gamepad connects or a key is pressed, the notice is replaced by the normal title at once.\n- In an AI Hub session on a touch-only device, call `AIHub.fail('Controller or keyboard required')`.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Systems\n\nEach system lists its rules, its numbers (**Piste** difficulty unless stated) and its tests. Speeds are world units\nper second (16 = 1 m).\n\n### 7.1 Rails and movement (`11-rails.js`)\n\n**Graph.** Nodes `{ id, at, kind: junction | room7 | exit | door | stop | lift | npc }` and edges\n`{ id, a, b, kind, path (E.Path, catmull), oneWay, gate, cams, tags }`. A walk edge is two-way. Ski, lift and jump","numLines":530,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2135}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Tmofv4zdhhvphwhpUvzKq7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
