{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghmopDHpSEF9d3UegyE8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"SKILLER7 is built: `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-020620.html` (about 1.9 MB) sits in the folder root, with `exit-interview.json` beside it (it parses and every field is filled). The final full test run is 193 passed, 0 failed. That includes three complete bot playthroughs of the whole demo, from the cold open to the credits: Piste and Bunny Hill played without cheats, and Off-Piste with god mode on to check for softlocks.\n\n**What it plays like.** Seven killers in one body, Oma Holle's family, share one snowbound micro-state. You hold a button to run fixed rails, picking a way at each junction, while the camera hard-cuts between fixed shots. Then you stop and aim in first person, scan for the laughing invisible Whiteouts and shoot their warm spot. The demo has two targets across ten areas: the hotel lobby, its third floor and service shaft, the village, the Palais des Neiges, the Black Run, the gondola, the Glacier Archive, the summit, plus Room 7 (save clock, Wax Bench upgrades, the Wardrobe). There are three bosses: Bellcaptain Grau, the Jolly Four (shot only while their own instrument plays) and Hansi Pulver in three phases. Persona-gated puzzles, the Sweep's rescue of frozen family, dev mode, attract mode, Echo Run and AI Hub ghost sessions are all in.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- **Changes from the plan.** All of TASK.md's features are in, but some of the plan's content is thinner than written:\n  - Most camera shots are placed automatically wherever there's a clear line to the hero, not hand-authored.\n  - The Black Run and the gondola ride are shorter than planned.\n  - Echo Run's ranks were set to match the shorter course.\n  - The bot finishes the demo in about 13 minutes of game time; a first-time player will take longer, but likely well under the plan's 45–60 minutes.\n- **Engine bug I worked around.** The 3D module's first-person overlay turns the world black when combined with the Comic cel filter. I draw the gun in the world instead, so it gets the same cel shading and ink, but it can dip into a wall at point-blank range. The 3D module itself is unchanged.\n- **Six engine patches.** All in `my-3d2dge.js`, each marked `S7 PATCH`; the engine's own template test and all 22 of its kit tests still pass with them.\n- **What I couldn't check.** Everything was tested in headless Chromium with software rendering, at roughly 10 frames a second. I haven't seen it on real GPU hardware, a real controller or a TV, and I haven't judged the audio by ear. The game loads three.js and Rapier from jsdelivr, so the single file needs a connection the first time it's opened; the tests use local copies.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 02:06:20\n- **Source:** Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:**\n  - Genre: an on-rails action-adventure shooter (rail movement, first-person scan-and-shoot, persona-switch puzzles, boss fights).\n  - Perspective: third-person rail cameras that hard-cut while you move; first person while aiming; a top-down schematic map.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the 2D engine, its modules and its 3D module), three.js r182 (WebGPU renderer with a WebGL 2 fallback), Rapier 0.19.3, AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native style: a PS1-resolution picture, pixel-art characters drawn on cards, the Comic cel filter. White snow, black shadows, saturated red. New hats, face accessories, held weapons, about 45 new objects and about 40 pen-drawn set pieces.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural rigs, driven every step and extended with the new gear. First-person weapons with kick and reload motion. Moving set pieces: a heaving featherbed, a falling elevator car, swinging bells, a band playing on the beat, a laughing snowman.\n- **Audio:** all in code. Music in the engine's step notation with danger layers; the band boss's four tracks are its weak points. Chip-synth sound effects, a positional laughter synth, and Animalese-style gibberish voices typed along with every line.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode. Echo Run is a 1–4 player race down the Black Run with the other players shown as echoes; on its own it races your recorded best run.\n- **Testing:**\n  - One command (`node tests/run.mjs`) that prints only failures and a count.\n  - 26 headless Node suites, including the bot playthroughs on three difficulties.\n  - Browser suites on the built file: boot, keyboard play, the pause menu and dev sliders, mouse picks, DualSense and Xbox prompts, attract mode, the touch-only notice.\n  - The AI Hub harness in three scenarios: 2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter and packet loss; the host leaving.\n  - Screenshots only for the look.\n- **Research:** none beyond the files provided (PLAN.md, the engine's handoff docs, the AI Hub README).\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 40 distinct issues. Most came from the bot playthrough and its isolated boss runs; the rest from the browser suites and screenshots.\n2. **How many were critical / game breaking?** About 12:\n   - The walker jumping to the far end of an edge.\n   - A cold open that waited 50 minutes.\n   - Three unbeatable boss phases: Grau's adds, the snowman's warm spots hidden inside its body, and a slalom with no retry.\n   - A cornice puzzle target that couldn't be hit.\n   - Frozen personas lost for good in areas you can't return to.\n   - Three crashes: a bad music value, a missing game state on the title, a disposed 3D world.\n   - An AI Hub session that opened on the title screen.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The headless bot playthrough of the whole demo (13 minutes of game time in about 7 seconds). It found every softlock and balance wall, one after another.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The content validator. It hasn't failed since I wrote it, because the playthrough had already caught everything it checks.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** The split between a deterministic fixed-step 2D engine and a 3D world drawn under it: all the game rules run headless in Node, and the rigs, the cel filter and the pen gave a strong look quickly.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - Stair tiles each rise their full height, which made a sawtooth across three tiles.\n   - The height conventions for the pen and `world.shape`.\n   - Which materials exist (there's no brass).\n   - The pixel font has no umlauts, accents, em dash or PlayStation button symbols.\n   - The first-person overlay doesn't work with the cel filter.\n   - Headless Chromium runs the 3D page at about 45 steps a second, which broke tests timed in steps.\n3. **Most important thing you had to build yourself?** The rail system (walker, junctions, quick turns, ski physics, lifts, jumps) and an automatic camera director that places fixed Killer7-style shots.\n4. **Hardest part about using the engine?** Keeping two copies of the level geometry in step: the sim keeps its own grid for line of sight and paths because the 3D world only exists in the browser.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** Six patches in `my-3d2dge.js`; the 3D module is unchanged.\n   - New hats: beanie, ushanka, felt, beret, hard hat, race helmet, pillbox.\n   - Face accessories: goggles, moustache, beard, shades, cigar, visor.\n   - Gun-like held weapons: revolver, pistol, rifle, shotgun, ski poles, alphorn, camera, launcher.\n   - PlayStation button symbols by default.\n   - `game.pauseBlock()`, so an open screen, or the AI Hub's Create + Options, never pauses the game.\n   - Font glyphs: umlauts, ß, accented letters, em dash, middle dot.\n6. **One thing you'd change?** A headless stand-in for the 3D world (level, rays and projection without three.js), so a game's rules and its picture could share one source of geometry in tests.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** It's concrete about deliverables and process (one test command, headless tools, a shared machine) while leaving the design open, and the plan gave a complete design to build to.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan's file names use a different model and date. STAGE.md says TASK.md wins, but that's easy to miss.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** Scope: nine areas, three bosses and every system at full depth, all built in one session.\n4. **What would you change?** Say how long the demo should take a first-time player, and whether a bot finishing it without cheats counts as proof that it's complete.\n\nThe source, tools and tests are in `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-020620/`; its README lists the build and test commands."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1234,"cache_read_input_tokens":849783,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1234},"output_tokens":735,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a19d4130-5ad5-4253-b2f9-ab1e00f8ce04","uuid":"dcfe0ed3-84f2-4a73-bf6e-0186f8209934","timestamp":"2026-11-04T11:01:43.225Z","request_id":"req_011CghmoopUpMQv7qwAARPfy"}
