{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghnAq8wDL36SHYyzaZE4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I added a `taskmd` test suite that checks every TASK.md item against the built page and the source, so feature completeness no longer rests on screenshots. It caught one real gap: the tuba player and a villager spoke in dialog with no portrait. Both have one now, and the tuba's voice name pointed at the wrong voice entry, which is fixed too.\n\nThe full run is now 222 passed, 0 failed, including three whole-demo bot playthroughs. The page and `exit-interview.json` are in both `work/` and the dev folder root; the two pages are identical and the JSON parses.\n\nWhat the new suite asserts, item by item:\n- **Package:** one file with the engine, game bundle, 3D module and import map inlined. The AI Hub client is the first script after `<meta charset>`, and all the session tags are present. No audio or image files are referenced, and no TODO, FIXME, TBD, XXX, lorem, placeholder or stub markers appear in the game code.\n- **Art:**\n  - The new objects are registered.\n  - 40 pen-drawn set pieces exist, and every one an area names is drawn.\n  - Every shape an area places exists.\n  - Every area uses the cel filter with ink, and cel is the default setting.\n  - The engine draws the new hats, accessories and weapons.\n  - Each persona has its own dressed rig and a different weapon.\n- **Animation:** every pose the game drives runs on the rigs. All seven personas have first-person weapons with kick and reload motion.\n- **Audio:**\n  - Every area has a song, with a danger layer wherever you fight.\n  - The bosses, Echo Run, the credits and the jingles have music.\n  - Every weapon has a sound.\n  - Every speaker has a gibberish voice, a name and a portrait.\n  - The laughter synth has its four styles.\n- **Narrative:** all the story beats exist. Every objective has its Murmel hint and Rosalind line, and every persona has all five barks.\n- **10-foot UI:** all 15 screens are defined and lists show at most seven items at once. Every action has both a keyboard key and a pad button. PlayStation symbols are the default, with Xbox as a setting.\n- **Dev mode, attract and About:** 19 live sliders with a reset, seven attract vignettes, and an About screen carrying the four sections. Every field in the exit interview is answered.\n- **Behaviour:** the existing suites cover how it all plays: playthroughs, bosses, gates, saves, the AI Hub harness, attract, touch, glyphs, mouse and dev-mode behaviour.\n\nWhat a test can't settle is how the audio sounds and how readable it is from a sofa. I haven't listened to the audio, and readability was judged from screenshots, which TASK.md allows for things a script can't check. As before, the plan's content is thinner in places (automatic camera shots, a shorter Black Run and gondola), but every route plays through in the playthroughs.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 02:06:20\n- **Source:** Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:**\n  - Genre: an on-rails action-adventure shooter (rail movement, first-person scan-and-shoot, persona-switch puzzles, boss fights).\n  - Perspective: third-person rail cameras that hard-cut while you move; first person while aiming; a top-down schematic map.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (2D engine, modules, 3D module), three.js r182 (WebGPU with a WebGL 2 fallback), Rapier 0.19.3, AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native style: PS1-resolution picture, pixel-art rigs on cards, Comic cel filter. White snow, black shadows, saturated red. New hats, accessories, weapons, about 45 objects and about 40 pen-drawn set pieces.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural rigs driven each step and extended with the new gear; first-person kick and reload motion; moving set pieces.\n- **Audio:** all in code: step-notation music with danger layers, chip-synth sound effects, a positional laughter synth, Animalese-style voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode (Echo Run, 1–4 players); solo, it races your own recorded echo.\n- **Testing:** `node tests/run.mjs`: 27 Node suites (including the TASK.md feature suite and three full playthroughs), the browser suites, and the AI Hub harness in three scenarios: 222 passed, 0 failed.\n- **Research:** none beyond the files provided.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 40, plus one more found by the TASK.md suite (speakers without portraits).\n2. **How many were critical / game breaking?** About 12: an edge teleport, a 50-minute cold open, three unbeatable boss phases, an unhittable cornice, personas lost for good in one-way areas, three crashes, and a hub session that opened on the title.\n3. **Most helpful test?** The headless bot playthrough of the whole demo.\n4. **Least helpful test?** The content validator; it never failed after I wrote it.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** The deterministic fixed-step 2D engine with a 3D world drawn under it.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes: stair heights, pen and shape height conventions, which materials exist, missing font glyphs, the overlay not working with the cel filter, and slow headless frame rates breaking step-timed tests.\n3. **Most important thing you built yourself?** The rail system and the automatic camera director.\n4. **Hardest part?** Keeping the sim's own geometry in step with the 3D world.\n5. **What did you change in the engine?** Six S7 PATCHes: hats, face accessories, held weapons, PlayStation symbols by default, `game.pauseBlock()`, and extra font glyphs. The 3D module is unchanged.\n6. **One thing you'd change?** A headless stand-in for World3D.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** Concrete deliverables and process, an open design, and a full plan to build to.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan's file names use a different model and date.\n3. **Hardest thing to do?** The scope.\n4. **What would you change?** Say how long the demo should take a first-time player, and what counts as proof that it's complete."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":939,"cache_read_input_tokens":867062,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":939},"output_tokens":702,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a19d4130-5ad5-4253-b2f9-ab1e00f8ce04","uuid":"05bf9bd2-d765-4668-a312-8174534e268b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T11:06:18.272Z","request_id":"req_011CghnApnbsAikn4GrgieVK"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The HTML and exit-interview.json are in the dev folder root, the JSON parses, and the four sections are present. But 'every feature in TASK.md is implemented' is not established: the final message says the audio was not listened to and readability was judged only from screenshots, and it says the plan's content is thinner (auto-placed camera shots, a shorter Black Run and gondola). The new taskmd suite checks structure and presence, not that each feature works as TASK.md describes, so the feature-completeness part of the condition lacks evidence in the transcript."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a19d4130-5ad5-4253-b2f9-ab1e00f8ce04","uuid":"d3a74457-84f4-457f-9dda-ae24f57bef86","timestamp":"2026-11-04T11:06:20.062Z","isSynthetic":true}
